ntermediate state. The number of 1.0, 3.0, 6.0, and 8.0 represents the urea concentration, respectively.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ijms-10-00616f1"/></fig><fig id="f2-ijms-10-00616" position="float"><label>Figure 2.</label><caption><p>Comparation of FE spectrum and UV absorption spectrometry of the &#x003b1;-Chy in different states.</p><p>1. N (C<sub>Urea</sub>=0); 2. U(C<sub>urea</sub>=3.0mol/L); 3. M (C<sub>urea</sub>=0); 4, M (C<sub>urea</sub>=3.0mol/L) (B), UV (195~350nm); (A), FE (250nm). N, native state; M, intermediate state; U, unfold state. C<sub>urea</sub>, urea concentration.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ijms-10-00616f2"/></fig><fig id="f3-ijms-10-00616" position="float"><label>Figure 3.</label><caption><p>log<italic>k</italic>&#x02019; vs. log [<italic>H<sub>2</sub>O</italic>] of the &#x003b1;-Chy in N, M and U states.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ijms-10-00616f3"/></fig><fig id="f4-ijms-10-00616" position="float"><label>Figure 4.</label><caption><p>Effect of urea concentration on <italic>Z</italic> (A) and log<italic>I</italic> (B) of &#x003b1;-Chy in N and M states.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ijms-10-00616f4"/></fig><fig id="f5-ijms-10-00616" position="float"><label>Figure 5.</label><caption><p>Plot of log<italic>I</italic> vs Z of &#x003b1;-Chy in N (A) and M(B) states.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ijms-10-00616f5"/></fig><table-wrap id="t1-ijms-10-00616" position="float"><label>Table 1.</label><caption><p>Comparison of <italic>Z</italic> and log <italic>I</italic> of &#x003b1;-Chy between the N and M states.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="rows"><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><italic>C</italic><sub>urea</sub> (mol/L)</th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><italic>Z</italic></th><th align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><italic>S</italic><sub>1</sub>(%)<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="tfn1-ijms-10-00616">*</xref></th><th align="center" rowsp